The Cartesian coordinate equation of the circle with center $A(0,2)$ and radius $2$ is:
\begin{align*}
(x - 0)^2 + (y - 2)^2 &= 2^2 \\
x^2 + y^2 - 4y &= 0
\end{align*}

To convert this equation into polar coordinates, we use the following relationships between Cartesian and polar coordinates:
\begin{align*}
x &= ρ\cos θ \\
y &= ρ\sin θ
\end{align*}

Substituting these expressions into the Cartesian equation, we obtain:
\begin{align*}
ρ^2\cos^2 θ + ρ^2\sin^2 θ - 4ρ\sin θ &= 0 \\
ρ^2(\cos^2 θ + \sin^2 θ) - 4ρ\sin θ &= 0 \\
ρ^2 - 4ρ\sin θ &= 0 \quad (\text{since } \cos^2 θ + \sin^2 θ = 1)
\end{align*}

Solving for $ρ$, we find:
\begin{align*}
ρ(ρ - 4\sin θ) &= 0 \\
ρ &= 0 \quad \text{or} \quad ρ = 4\sin θ
\end{align*}

Since $ρ = 0$ represents the origin, which is not part of the given circle, the polar coordinate equation of the circle is:
$$\boxed{ρ = 4\sin θ}$$

